      "lineNbr": 6433,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-19T05:09:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. Not just the configuration, this also doesn\u0027t know the original NetworkScore. I could have made a NetworkScore from a FullScore and used makeProspectiveScore instead, but this seemed more straightforward.\n\n2. It\u0027s kind of a variation of mixInScore in usage, but not really in principle. mixInScore is about mixing the CS-managed capabilities, and it\u0027s named after mixInCapabilities and other functions in CS. This is the with* pattern, which is the standard way of calling a function on an immutable object that will return a similar object with one or more differences.\n\nSo I don\u0027t think we should call that one \"mixIn...\" because it doesn\u0027t do what our other mixIn functions do.\n\n If we take the problem the other way around and name them after their usage instead of naming them after what they do, in which case both of them would be something like updateFor... or something. But I think naming should follow what it *does* rather than how it\u0027s used in almost all cases. How it\u0027s used is the caller\u0027s business, and when you read the code and check the logic it lets you understand how it\u0027s implemented. Case in point, here if we said \"updateFor...\", you wouldn\u0027t know what is updated so it would be harder to understand.",
      "parentUuid": "23e07b32_f257a568",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6433,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 6433,
        "endChar": 62
      },
      "revId": "8240acc2bd5cd18a3164fbd70da4e1c6007a68e3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
